     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Hi,</div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Wed, 15 Dec 2021 at 14:11, Ícaro Agostino &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">icaroa...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Dear Simmer developers,</div><div><br></div><div>What is the difference between the <b>get_queue_size()</b> and <b>get_queue_count()</b> functions? In the official documentation on Simmer website there is no specific explanation for each one. In my current model I need to know during the simulation run the queue size of a resource to make a decision on a previous trajectory. The function I should use to access the current queue size during simulation is <b>get_queue_count()</b>?</div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div><ul><li>get_queue_size retrieves the number of &quot;seats&quot; in the queue. The capacity of the queue. This is typically constant, but it may change dynamically if you provided a schedule or you change it using set_queue_size().</li><li>get_queue_count retrieves the number of arrivals in the queue. So size - count gives you the number of vacancies.</li></ul><div>So I understand you are interested in get_queue_count.</div></div><div><br></div><div>Iñaki</div><div> </div><div><br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"></blockquote></div></div>"
    ],
    [
